 "question": "Invariant 17 asks where information dies: prediction -> selection -> construction -> holding/exit. The construction tax has only ever been measured historically here. Where does it sit on the book that is about to trade, TODAY?",
 "method": "The 2026-09-08 sealed book (sha f20929777f77fa55) states a target weight per name (`max_notional_each`). A DRY `scripts.run_pass` was run for hack3 and hack6 with the real sizer, the real admission gates and the real risk guards, and the share counts it would submit were recorded. Live notional = shares x last close. The transfer ratio is live notional / sealed notional per name. No orders were placed.",
 "answer": "For these two books the SIZER LOSES NOTHING. Transfer ratio mean 1.00, sd 0.00, range 0.99-1.00 on both books; gross 83.0% -> 82.9% (hack3) and 90.0% -> 89.7% (hack6). The residual is share rounding, not construction.",
 "so_what": "This LOCALISES the leak. Construction is not where these books lose information, so the four-stage chain's loss for the fleet sits in SELECTION (which names the seal picks) or in HOLDING/EXIT. That is consistent with the same day's other measurement: the stop sat at 0.98 daily sd on hack6 and stopped 56.3% of entries out before session 10 of a 21-session thesis (RECEIPT_2026-09-07_STOP_WIDTH_VS_HOLD.json). The exit stage is where to look.",
 "important_caveat": "This is a ONE-DAY, TWO-BOOK, DRY measurement of the sizing step alone. It says nothing about fills, slippage or costs, which are a separate and unmeasured part of construction. A ratio of 1.00 here does NOT mean the realised book will match the seal -- it means the code that turns a weight into a share count is faithful.",
